  1504	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  1505	# PLAN162_FIX_S2 — the sentinel SET is a trust anchor (consensus S2)
  1506	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  1507	# ``.claude/plans/PLAN-*/**/approved.md`` is guarded by NEITHER rail, and
  1508	# under an Owner ``CEO_SENTINEL_UNLOCK`` window the GPG rail is skipped
  1509	# entirely — so an ``approved.md`` the AGENT writes mid-window GRANTS.
  1510	# That is a bypass of AUTHORIZATION, not merely a DoS on the gate's cost.
  1511	#
  1512	# The first mitigation drafted for this ("tracked in git OR pre-existing")
  1513	# was DEFEATED by the pair-rail in one command: inside the window the
  1514	# agent writes the file and runs an ordinary ``git add``; ``ls-files
  1515	# --error-unmatch`` then calls it TRACKED, and a HEAD-based check would
  1516	# accept an agent-authored COMMIT just the same. **Git state is a
  1517	# statement about the index, not about who authorized the file.**
  1518	#
  1519	# The trust anchor is therefore SESSION START: under ``env_override``,
  1520	# accept only a sentinel that already existed in the tree at the commit
  1521	# the Owner names in ``CEO_SESSION_ANCHOR_SHA``. The anchor is an ENV
  1522	# var, which is exactly the same trust primitive the unlock itself rests
  1523	# on (a sub-agent cannot set env in the parent shell) — so requiring it
  1524	# adds no new assumption. No anchor => no proof => no grant.
  1525	#
  1526	# ## PLAN162_FIX_S2R2 — what the codex round-2 pair-rail corrected
  1527	#
  1528	# The first draft of this rail proved only that the sentinel PATH existed
  1529	# at the anchor, then parsed the CURRENT bytes. Codex defeated it in one
  1530	# move (P1-1): inside a legitimate window, take any pre-existing
  1531	# unguarded ``approved.md``, keep its ``Approved-By:`` line, ADD a target
  1532	# to ``Scope:`` — path-existence still holds, GPG is skipped, and the
  1533	# hook authorizes off attacker-modified bytes. **A trust decision must be
  1534	# made on the ANCHORED bytes, not on bytes that merely live at an
  1535	# anchored path.** ``git cat-file blob`` now supplies the text that is
  1536	# parsed; what is on disk is never consulted on this path.
  1537	#
  1538	# Two more round-2 corrections ride the same fold:
  1539	#
  1540	#   * P1-2 — the git subprocess is bounded by the REMAINING wall budget
  1541	#     (``_git_anchor_timeout``), exactly as the gpg spawn already is. A
  1542	#     5 s probe inside a 4 s budget lets the harness kill the process
  1543	#     before it emits the intended BLOCK, which is the zero-emit
  1544	#     fail-open this whole patch exists to remove.
  1545	#   * P1-3 — a fail-closed gate with no recovery route is a brick. The
  1546	#     anchor alone bricks the ONE use ADR-119 names for the unlock
  1547	#     ("ceremony authoring where the .asc is being signed in parallel"):
  1548	#     a freshly authored sentinel predates nothing. So the Owner may
  1549	#     instead PIN the sentinel's content hash
  1550	#     (``CEO_SENTINEL_UNLOCK_SHA256``, space/comma-separated for several).
  1551	#     It rests on the same trust primitive as the unlock itself — env a
  1552	#     sub-agent cannot set in the parent shell — and it is strictly
  1553	#     stronger than the anchor: it binds CONTENT, not a path. Reading the
  1554	#     value gains an attacker nothing (sha256 preimage), and it works in
  1555	#     a non-git tree, where the anchor cannot.
  1556	#
  1557	# ## RESIDUAL — read before ratifying
  1558	#
  1559	# The ANCHOR rail arms only when the project is a git work tree (``.git``
  1560	# present); otherwise no anchor proof exists and the legacy accept
  1561	# stands. Consequence: ``rm -rf .git`` disarms the anchor half. Named
  1562	# here rather than buried. The digest-pin half is unaffected by it — it
  1563	# is the git-free proof form the first draft said "is a separate
  1564	# decision", and this is that decision.
  1565	#
  1566	# DECIDED, not overlooked: in a NON-git tree a digest pin does not make
  1567	# the unpinned sentinels stricter — a pin-miss falls through to the
  1568	# residual accept there, exactly as it falls through to the anchor rail
  1569	# in a git tree. Making the pin authoritative in a non-git tree would
  1570	# mean "pin one file, reject every other sentinel in the pack" in the
  1571	# one tree shape that has no second proof form: the C3 self-DoS class,
  1572	# traded for a tightening that only bites where the legacy accept
  1573	# already applies.
  1574	#
  1575	# Landing this CHANGES a documented operator contract (ADR-119,
  1576	# SPEC/v1/sentinel-format.schema.md, docs/FAQ.md, the READMEs): an
  1577	# unlock window now needs a THIRD value. Those surfaces are updated in
  1578	# this same patch, and ``_unlock_trust_hint`` teaches both forms in the
  1579	# block reason itself — a required value that only exists in the diff is
  1580	# the lockout P1-3 named. Owner ratification: ADR-164-AMEND-1.
  1581	_SESSION_ANCHOR_ENV = "CEO_SESSION_ANCHOR_SHA"
  1582	_UNLOCK_DIGEST_ENV = "CEO_SENTINEL_UNLOCK_SHA256"
  1583	_ANCHOR_SHA_RE = re.compile(r"^[0-9a-fA-F]{7,64}$")
  1584	_SHA256_RE = re.compile(r"^[0-9a-fA-F]{64}$")
  1585	_GIT_ANCHOR_TIMEOUT_CAP_S = 5.0
  1586	# (repo_root, anchor, rel) -> anchored text, or None for "proven absent".
  1587	# A key is absent from the map when no VERDICT was reached (budget
  1588	# refusal, git fault) — errors are never memoized, same contract as
  1589	# ``_SIG_VERIFY_CACHE``.
  1590	_ANCHOR_BLOB_CACHE = {}  # type: Dict[tuple, Optional[str]]
  1591	
  1592	# Why the unlock rail refused, for the block reason. First refusal wins;
  1593	# reset per ``decide()`` call.
  1594	_UNLOCK_TRUST_DIAG = None  # type: Optional[str]
